# G020 — Count Words in a String

*v2. See `count-words.md` for prose: word-level granularity between
character (G018) and document, word frequency confirms `@breakdown`
as a generic primitive parameterised by granularity, diversity ratio
as content-quality metric.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Editorial Quality Analysis

[editorial-quality @text
    IN ORDER TO
        measure text's diversity and average length concurrently
        combine into a readability score
        and pass the `where` only if the combined score clears a threshold

    concurrent [
        diversity: @d  <- [calc {expr: "[unique-words {text: @text}] / " +
                                        "[word-count {text: @text}]"}]
    |
        length:    @l  <- [average-word-length {text: @text}]
    ]
    join

    @score <- [calc {expr: "(@d * 100) - (@l * 5)"}]
    @score
        where at least 40 -> #editorial-pass
        otherwise          -> #needs-revision
]

---

## Verb Surface

[word-count
    {text}                                   ;; -> Int
]

[word-frequency
    {text}                                    ;; -> {word → count}
]

[unique-words
    {text}                                    ;; -> Int
]

[average-word-length
    {text}                                    ;; -> Float
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: `@breakdown` generic `where` granularity parameter

[breakdown {text: @t, by: @level}]
    @level
        where "character"  -> count per codepoint
        where "word"        -> count per whitespace-delimited token
        where "sentence"    -> count per terminator-delimited segment
    return distribution of (unit → count)
    IN ORDER TO
        make G018's vowel breakdown and G020's word frequency
        the same operation at different granularities
]

Same primitive scales from characters to words to paragraphs. The
granularity parameter is the generalisation.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [word-count {text: "the quick brown fox the fox"}]     ;; -> 6
    -> [unique-words {text: "the quick brown fox the fox"}]   ;; -> 4
    -> [word-frequency {text: "the quick brown fox the fox"}]
    ;; -> {the: 2, quick: 1, brown: 1, fox: 2}
    -> [average-word-length {text: "the quick brown fox"}]    ;; -> 4.25
]
